--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

InterLock depends on third-party Python packages distributed under their own
licenses. `make license-report` installs exactly what the published image
installs (`requirements-production.txt`) and writes the per-package inventory
to `build/release-evidence/licenses.{json,md}`; it fails if any package's
license falls outside the position stated here. `make sbom` writes a CycloneDX
bill of materials for the same set.

That position:

  - Permissive licenses (Apache-2.0, MIT, BSD, ISC, PSF, Zlib, CC0 and
    similar) make up almost all of it.
  - LGPL-3.0: PyGithub and python-gitlab, described in the next section.
  - MPL-2.0 (file-level copyleft): certifi, tqdm, and orjson (orjson is
    Apache-2.0 OR MIT with MPL-2.0 files). They are installed as unmodified
    upstream distributions from PyPI, where their source is published; MPL-2.0
    obligations attach only to modified MPL-covered files, and none are
    modified.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Copyleft components (included in the published image)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following dependencies are licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public
License v3.0 (LGPL-3.0). They are part of the `connectors-repo` extra, which
the `production` and `production-worker` extras include, and they are therefore
present in the published container image and in `requirements-production.txt`:

  - PyGithub          LGPL-3.0    https://github.com/PyGithub/PyGithub
  - python-gitlab     LGPL-3.0    https://github.com/python-gitlab/python-gitlab

They were previously excluded so that the published image carried no copyleft
dependency. That made the GitHub and GitLab connectors unusable in the only
image most people run: the adapters raise `PyGithub is not installed`, so a
documented capability could not work as shipped. Including the libraries was
judged the more honest of the two positions.

InterLock's own license is unaffected. This project remains Apache-2.0, and
using an LGPL-3.0 library does not place InterLock under the LGPL: the LGPL
exists precisely to permit that combination.

How the LGPL-3.0 obligations are met:

  - The libraries are installed as unmodified upstream distributions from
    PyPI, and their complete corresponding source is published there and at
    the URLs above. Neither library is modified, embedded, or statically
    linked; InterLock imports them dynamically at runtime.
  - The image stores them as ordinary Python packages in `site-packages`, so a
    recipient may replace either with a modified version - for example
    `pip install --target` over the installed copy, or a derived image layer -
    and InterLock will use the replacement, since it imports by module name.
  - `requirements-production.txt` records the exact versions shipped.

Anyone redistributing this image, or a derivative of it, carries the same
obligations and should reproduce this notice.

If you need a build with no copyleft dependency, install the `production`
extra's components without `connectors-repo`. The GitHub and GitLab source
connectors then fail closed rather than degrading silently, and nothing else
changes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Optional model and runtime components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The `pii`, `ml`, `vector`, and `ingestion` extras pull in additional runtimes
(Presidio, spaCy, sentence-transformers, PyTorch, FAISS) under their own
permissive licenses. Machine-learning model weights downloaded at runtime are
governed by the license of the individual model and are not distributed with
this project.

